This example shows that the arrangement from Newton's second provides a better interpretation of the Lagrange multipliers function.
Keeping mass times acceleration on the left-hand side and the force on the right-hand side, is a useful way to define the system of equations and solve it as
$$
\boxed{
\left\{
  \begin{array}{l}
    \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} t} \frac{\partial}{\partial \dot{x}} \mathcal{L}
    - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \mathcal{L}
    = \lambda(t) \frac{\partial}{\partial x} f \\
    \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} t} \frac{\partial}{\partial \dot{y}} \mathcal{L}
    - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathcal{L}
    = \lambda(t) \frac{\partial}{\partial y} f \\
    f(x,y) - \text{const} = 0
  \end{array}
\right.
}.
$$

